hypotheses.statement:
Let $d,m\ge1$ and $p\ge2$ be integers, and let $\beta>m+p-2$, $H,R>0$, and $z\in\mathbb R^d$. Suppose that $\mu$ is a finite nonnegative Borel measure supported on $B(z,R)$ and satisfying $$\label{h:dv:ambient-bound}
 \mu(B(x,r))\le Hr^\beta
 \qquad(x\in\mathbb R^d,\ r>0).$$ For $0\le j\le p-2$, put $$J_j=1+3^j2^\beta\frac{m}{\beta-j-m},$$ and define $$\label{h:dv:moment-scale}
 \mathcal B=(2\pi)^{-m(p-1)/(2p)}
 H R^{\beta-m(1-1/p)}
 \left(\prod_{j=0}^{p-2}J_j\right)^{1/p}.$$

$$\label{h:proj:growth}
 \nu(B(t,\rho))\le\rho^a
 \qquad(t\in\operatorname{supp}\nu,\ \rho>0).$$

Let $d\ge2$, let $\pi$ be a Borel probability measure on $S^{d-1}$, and let $X\sim\gamma_1=N(0,I_d)$ be independent of $S\sim\pi$. Suppose that $$\label{h:stream:pair-domination}
 \mathcal L(X,\langle X,S\rangle)\ll
 \lambda,\qquad \lambda(dx,dy)=\gamma_1(dx)\,dy$$ on $\mathbb R^d\times\mathbb R$. Fix an integer horizon $T\ge0$ and finite state sets at every sample index. For each state and index, suppose every coordinate of the finite vector of transition and stopping probabilities is measurable in the $\lambda$-completion of the Borel sigma field. A terminal output law is a Borel probability measure on $S^{d-1}$ indexed only by its terminal state and stopping index. Decisions made without a new sample also depend only on the state and index.*

Let $(E,\mathcal E,\lambda)$ be a measure space, let $\mathcal D\in\mathcal E$ have finite measure, let $p>1$, let $U\ge0$, and let $N\ge1$ be an integer. Suppose finite nonnegative measures $\eta_1,\ldots,\eta_N$ are supported on $\mathcal D$, and that for every measurable $0\le g\le1$, $$\label{h:alt:test}
 \int g\,d\eta_j
 \le U\left(\int_{\mathcal D}g\,d\lambda\right)^{1-1/p}
 \qquad(1\le j\le N).$$

Let $k$ be an integer with $1\le k\le p$, and let a Borel function $f:Q\to[0,1]$ satisfy <a href="#h:cube:local" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:cube:local">[h:cube:local]</a> with $L\ge0$. For $z\in\mathbb R^n$ and $r>0$, define the finite joint measure $$\label{h:cube:joint-measure}
 \eta_{f,z,r}(E)
 =\int\!\int_{\mathcal B(z,r)}
       \mathbf 1_E(A,A\iota(u))f(u)\,du\,\gamma_k(dA)$$ for Borel $E\subseteq\mathbb R^{k\times d}\times\mathbb R^k$.

outputs.exact_statement:
For $d\ge2$, $z\in S^{d-1}$, and $r>0$, $$\label{h:local:cap}
 \sigma_d(B(z,r))\le r^{d-1}.$$ Consequently, if $Q$ is any probability law on $S^{d-1}$ independent of the signal and $$h(s)=Q\{v:\arccos\langle v,s\rangle\le\epsilon\},$$ then $h$ has local mass at scale $R=\epsilon$: $$\label{h:local:terminal}
 \int_{B(z,r)}h\,d\sigma_d\le(\epsilon r)^{(d-1)/2}.$$

Let $d,m\ge1$ and $p\ge2$ be integers, and let $\beta>m+p-2$, $H,R>0$, and $z\in\mathbb R^d$. Suppose that $\mu$ is a finite nonnegative Borel measure supported on $B(z,R)$ and satisfying $$\label{h:dv:ambient-bound}
 \mu(B(x,r))\le Hr^\beta
 \qquad(x\in\mathbb R^d,\ r>0).$$ For $0\le j\le p-2$, put $$J_j=1+3^j2^\beta\frac{m}{\beta-j-m},$$ and define $$\label{h:dv:moment-scale}
 \mathcal B=(2\pi)^{-m(p-1)/(2p)}
 H R^{\beta-m(1-1/p)}
 \left(\prod_{j=0}^{p-2}J_j\right)^{1/p}.$$ Then $\eta_\mu$ has a nonnegative jointly Borel density $F$ with respect to $\lambda_m$, which can be chosen to vanish outside $\mathcal E_{z,R}$, and $$\label{h:dv:finite-density-bound}
 \|F\|_{L^p(\lambda_m)}\le\mathcal B.$$

Under the assumptions of Theorem <a href="#h:dv:finite-density" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="h:dv:finite-density">6</a>, use the factors $J_j$ defined there. If $L$ is an affine $j$-plane with $0\le j\le p-2$, then, for $0<t\le R$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mu\{s:\operatorname{dist}(s,L)\le t\}
 &\le 3^j2^\beta H R^j t^{\beta-j},
 \label{h:dv:tube}\\
 \int\operatorname{dist}(s,L)^{-m}\,d\mu(s)
 &\le J_j H R^{\beta-m}.
 \label{h:dv:inverse-distance}
\end{aligned}$$ For a tuple $(s_1,\ldots,s_p)$, let $V=[s_2-s_1,\ldots,s_p-s_1]$. The matrix $V$ has full column rank for $\mu^{\otimes p}$-almost every tuple, and $$\int\det(V^{\mathsf T}V)^{-m/2}\,d\mu^{\otimes p}
 \le
 H^p R^{\beta p-m(p-1)}
 \prod_{j=0}^{p-2}J_j.
 \label{h:dv:gram-product}$$ The integrand in <a href="#h:dv:gram-product" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:dv:gram-product">[h:dv:gram-product]</a> may be assigned any value on the null set of rank-deficient tuples.

Under the assumptions of Theorem <a href="#h:dv:finite-density" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="h:dv:finite-density">6</a>, use the scale $\mathcal B$ from <a href="#h:dv:moment-scale" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:dv:moment-scale">[h:dv:moment-scale]</a>. Let $\phi_\delta$ be the $N(0,\delta^2I_m)$ density and put $$F_\delta(A,y)=\int\phi_\delta(y-As)\,d\mu(s),
 \qquad \delta>0.$$ Then $F_\delta$ is jointly Borel, and, with $V=[s_2-s_1,\ldots,s_p-s_1]$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \int F_\delta^p\,d\lambda_m
 &=(2\pi)^{-m(p-1)/2}
   \int\det\!\left(
     V^{\mathsf T}V+
     \delta^2(I_{p-1}+\mathbf 1\mathbf 1^{\mathsf T})
   \right)^{-m/2}\,d\mu^{\otimes p}
 \notag\\
 &\le \mathcal B^p.
 \label{h:dv:gaussian-moment-bound}
\end{aligned}$$

Let $E$ be a Euclidean space, let $\lambda$ be a locally finite, sigma-finite Borel measure on $E$, and let $\eta$ be a finite nonnegative Borel measure on $E$. Suppose $p>1$, $U\ge0$, and $$\left|\int\psi\,d\eta\right|
 \le U\|\psi\|_{L^{p/(p-1)}(\lambda)}
 \qquad\text{for every real }\psi\in C_c(E).$$ Then $\eta=f\lambda$ for a nonnegative $f\in L^p(\lambda)$ with $\|f\|_p\le U$.

Use the parameters in <a href="#h:local:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:local:parameters">[h:local:parameters]</a>, and let $1\le m\le q$. Let $
u$ be a finite nonnegative Borel measure of mass at most one, supported on the unit ball in $\mathbb R^d$, such that $$\label{h:proj:growth}

u(B(t,ho))\leho^a
 \qquad(t\in\operatorname{supp}
u,\ ho>0).$$ The image of $\gamma_m(dA)
u(dt)$ under $(A,t)\mapsto(A,At)$ has a nonnegative jointly Borel density $f_
u$ relative to $\lambda_m$, and $$\label{h:proj:norm}
 \|f_
u\|_{L^q(\lambda_m)}\le\exp(Cd).$$ The constant is independent of $
u$ and $m$.

Let $1\le m\le d$, $z\in\mathbb R^d$, and $R>0$. Use the Borel set $\mathcal E_{z,R}$ from <a href="#h:dv:support-sets" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:dv:support-sets">[h:dv:support-sets]</a>. There is an absolute $C_{\mathrm E}\ge1$ such that, for a standard Gaussian $m$-by-$d$ matrix, $$\begin{aligned}
 \lambda_m(\mathcal E_{z,R})
 &=v_mR^m\mathbb E_A\sqrt{\det(AA^{\mathsf T})}
 \notag\\
 &\le v_mR^m d^{m/2}
 \le R^m(2\pi e d/m)^{m/2}
 \le C_{\mathrm E}^dR^m .
 \label{h:ev:ellipsoid-volume}
\end{aligned}$$ Every density of the exact image of a measure supported on $B(z,R)$ vanishes almost everywhere outside $\mathcal E_{z,R}$.

Let $1\le m\le q$ and $N\ge1$. Suppose the measurable functions $h_1,\ldots,h_N:S^{d-1}\to[0,1]$ have local mass at a common scale $R>0$, as in <a href="#h:local:bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:local:bound">[h:local:bound]</a>. Let $g_j:\mathbb R^{m\times d}\times\mathbb R^m\to[0,1]$ be Borel routing probabilities with $\sum_{j=1}^N g_j\le1$, and define $$h(s)=\sum_{j=1}^N h_j(s)\int g_j(A,As)\,d\gamma_m(A).$$ Then, for every $z\in S^{d-1}$ and $r>0$, $$\label{h:block:mass}
 \int_{B(z,r)}h\,d\sigma
 \le\exp(Cd)N^{1/q}(Rr)^a.$$ Equivalently, $h$ has local mass at scale $$\label{h:block:scale}
 R'=R\exp(Cd/a)N^{1/(aq)}.$$

For a fixed-seed Borel learner with the state sets, output laws, and phase convention just specified, and the Borel terminal score $\ell$, let $0\le t<T$, $u\in Q_t$, and $1\le m\le T-t$. There are an integer $$\label{h:stream:destinations}
 1\le N\le(m+2)2^M,$$ Borel routing functions $g_j:\mathbb R^{m\times d}\times\mathbb R^m\to[0,1]$ with $\sum_jg_j\le1$, and Borel functions $h_j:S^{d-1}\to[0,1]$ such that, for every $s\in S^{d-1}$, $$\label{h:stream:category-representation}
 h_{t,u}(s)=\sum_{j=1}^N h_j(s)
       \int g_j(A,As)\,d\gamma_m(A).$$ Here $\gamma_m$ is the law of an $m$-by-$d$ standard Gaussian matrix. Each destination is either a terminal state $w\in Q_k$ at an index $k\in\{t,\ldots,t+m\}$, with score function $$h^{\mathrm{out}}_{k,w}(s)
 =\int_{S^{d-1}}\ell(s,v)\,\mathsf Q_{k,w}(dv),$$ or a continuing state $w\in Q_{t+m}$ before its stopping check, with score function $h_{t+m,w}(s)$. The terminal output law $\mathsf Q_{k,w}$ depends only on its state and index. The value $g_j(A,b)$ is the probability of destination $j$ when the block data are $(A,b)$.

Let $d\ge1$ be an integer and $T\ge0$. Let $S$ be uniform on $S^{d-1}$. Let a matrix $A$ with $d$ columns, independent of $S$, have row span $U$ of dimension at most $T$. Suppose an output $\widehat S$ is drawn from a probability kernel depending only on $(A,AS)$. Put $u=P_US$ and $v=(I-P_U)S$. For an arbitrary output in $\mathbb R^d$ and $0<\epsilon<1$, $$\label{h:ev:direct-residual}
 \Pr\{\|\widehat S-S\|\le\epsilon\}
 \le\frac12+\frac{T}{2d(1-\epsilon^2)}.$$ More generally, for $0<b_0<1$ and $0\le\epsilon<\sqrt{1-b_0}$, $$\label{h:ev:antipodal}
 \Pr\{\|\widehat S-S\|\le\epsilon\}
 \le\frac12+\frac12\Pr\{\|u\|^2\ge b_0\}
 \le\frac12+\frac{T}{2b_0d}.$$ For a unit output one also has $$\label{h:ev:reflection-expectation}
 \mathbb E\langle\widehat S,v\rangle=0,\qquad
 \mathbb E\langle\widehat S,S\rangle
 \le\mathbb E\|u\|\le\sqrt{T/d}.$$

Let $(E,\mathcal E,\lambda)$ be a measure space, let $\mathcal D\in\mathcal E$ have finite measure, let $p>1$, let $U\ge0$, and let $N\ge1$ be an integer. Suppose finite nonnegative measures $\eta_1,\ldots,\eta_N$ are supported on $\mathcal D$, and that for every measurable $0\le g\le1$, $$\label{h:alt:test}
 \int g\,d\eta_j
 \le U\left(\int_{\mathcal D}g\,d\lambda\right)^{1-1/p}
 \qquad(1\le j\le N).$$ For measurable routes $g_j\ge0$ with $\sum_jg_j\le1$, $$\label{h:alt:selection-bound}
 \sum_{j=1}^N\int g_j\,d\eta_j
 \le N^{1/p}U\,\lambda(\mathcal D)^{1-1/p}.$$ A sufficient condition for <a href="#h:alt:test" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:alt:test">[h:alt:test]</a> is $\eta_j=f_j\lambda$, where $f_j\ge0$ vanishes outside $\mathcal D$ and $\|f_j\|_{L^p(\lambda)}\le U$.*


*In particular, if $r>0$, $m,\beta\in\mathbb R$, and positive constants $H,V,L$ satisfy $$U\le HLr^{\beta-m(1-1/p)},\qquad
 \lambda(\mathcal D)\le Vr^m,$$ then the right side of <a href="#h:alt:selection-bound" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:alt:selection-bound">[h:alt:selection-bound]</a> is at most $$\label{h:alt:scale-cancellation}
 N^{1/p}H V^{1-1/p}Lr^\beta.$$

Let $k$ be an integer with $1\le k\le p$, and let a Borel function $f:Q\to[0,1]$ satisfy <a href="#h:cube:local" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:cube:local">[h:cube:local]</a> with $L\ge0$. For $z\in\mathbb R^n$ and $r>0$, define the finite joint measure $$\label{h:cube:joint-measure}
 \eta_{f,z,r}(E)
 =\int\!\int_{\mathcal B(z,r)}
       \mathbf 1_E(A,A\iota(u))f(u)\,du\,\gamma_k(dA)$$ for Borel $E\subseteq\mathbb R^{k\times d}\times\mathbb R^k$. There is a universal $C_2\ge1$ such that $\eta_{f,z,r}=h_{f,z,r}\lambda_k$ for a nonnegative density with $$\label{h:cube:density-bound}
 \|h_{f,z,r}\|_{L^p(\lambda_k)}
 \le C_2^n Lr^{\beta-k(1-1/p)}.$$ If $\mathcal B(z,r)\ne\varnothing$, fix any $u_*\in\mathcal B(z,r)$ and set $$\label{h:cube:ellipsoid-support}
 \mathcal E_{u_*,r}
 =\{(A,y):y\in A\iota(u_*)+A B_d(0,4r)\}.$$ Every such density, for this same $z,r,u_*$, vanishes almost everywhere outside $\mathcal E_{u_*,r}$, and $$\label{h:cube:ellipsoid-volume}
 \lambda_k(\mathcal E_{u_*,r})\le C_2^n r^k.$$

Assume <a href="#h:dv:parameters" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="h:dv:parameters">[h:dv:parameters]</a>, and let $H,R>0$ and $z\in\mathbb R^d$. Let $f:S^{d-1}\to[0,1]$ be Borel measurable, let $\nu=f\sigma$, and suppose $\nu(B(x,r))\le H r^\beta$ for every $x\in\mathbb R^d$ and $r>0$. For $\mu=\mathbf 1_{B(z,R)}\nu$, the pushforward $A_\#\mu$ has, for almost every $A$, a Lebesgue density $F(A,\cdot)$. There is an absolute $C_{\mathrm D}\ge1$ for which a jointly measurable nonnegative choice of these densities satisfies $$\label{h:ev:density-moment}
 \int F(A,y)^p\,d\lambda_m(A,y)
 \le C_{\mathrm D}^{d(p-1)}
       H^pR^{\beta p-m(p-1)}.$$ The density represents the exact labels $As$.

Assume $d\ge16$, set $n=d-1$, $p=\lfloor d/8\rfloor$, and let $n/2\le\beta\le d/2$. Let $M,T\ge0$ be integers, $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, and consider a learner in Definition <a href="#h:model" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="h:model">1</a>. Under the uniform-sphere signal prior, write $P_{\mathrm{succ}}$ for its success probability with either angular or chordal error at most $\epsilon$, and put $b=\lceil T/p\rceil$. Then $$\label{h:ev:common-success}
 P_{\mathrm{succ}}\le
 \min\left\{1,\,
 4^n\epsilon^{n-\beta}
 \left[C_{\mathrm{step}}^d
       \bigl((p+2)2^M\bigr)^{1/p}\right]^b\right\}.$$ The same assertion holds with the initial factor $4^n$ replaced by the polar-cap constant $C_0^d$.

Assume $d\ge16$ and $p=\lfloor d/8\rfloor$. Let $M,T\ge0$ be integers, let $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, and consider a learner in Definition <a href="#h:model" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="h:model">1</a> with these parameters. Draw $S\sim\sigma$ independently of its Gaussian rows $x_1,\ldots,x_T$ and its data-independent randomness, and set the exact labels $y_t=\langle x_t,S\rangle$. Write $\Pr_\sigma$ for the resulting joint law and put $b=\lceil T/p\rceil$. With an absolute $C_{\mathrm{step}}\ge1$, the uniform-prior angular success probability satisfies $$\label{h:ev:half-sphere-success}
 \Pr_\sigma\{\operatorname{angle}(\widehat S,S)\le\epsilon\}
 \le
 \min\!\left\{1,\,
 4^d\epsilon^{(d-1)/2}
 \left[C_{\mathrm{step}}^d
       \bigl((p+2)2^M\bigr)^{1/p}\right]^b
 \right\}.$$ The uniform-prior chordal success probability satisfies $$\label{h:ev:half-ambient-success}
 \Pr_\sigma\{\|\widehat S-S\|\le\epsilon\}
 \le
 \min\!\left\{1,\,
 C_0^d\epsilon^{d/2-1}
 \left[C_{\mathrm{step}}^d
       \bigl((2p+4)2^M\bigr)^{1/p}\right]^b
 \right\}.$$ The second event contains the angular success event.

For all sufficiently large $d$, let $0\le T\le d/4$ be an integer. Let $S$ be uniform on $S^{d-1}$, let $X$ be an independent $T$-by-$d$ standard Gaussian matrix, and put $Y=XS$. For every $0<\epsilon\le1/10$, any possibly randomized estimator $W\in\mathbb R^d$ based on the entire pair $(X,Y)$ satisfies $$\label{h:end:full-data-bound}
 \mathbb P\{\|W-S\|\le\epsilon\}
 \le \frac13+\left(\frac25\right)^{3d/4-1}
 <\frac23.$$ Consequently, a learner with uniform-sphere angular success at least $2/3$ at accuracy $\epsilon\le1/10$ must have $T>d/4$, even with unlimited memory and access to all pre-generated samples.
